    743:| Bessel-basis PSLQ identity search for f(x) = π(x) − R(x) (deep-focus revisit of Task 3 / S29) | FAIL | E | Strict extension of S29's PSLQ identity search to a basis disjoint from the elementary/li/sin-cos basis tested in S29: 10-element basis {f(x), 1, log x, √x, li(x), K_0(log x), I_0(log x), J_0(γ_1·log x), Y_0(γ_1·log x), K_0(2π·√(log x))} — i.e., adds modified-Bessel K/I, oscillatory-Bessel J/Y at the first zeta zero, and the partition-asymptotic kernel K_0(2π√·) from the Hardy-Ramanujan circle method. Motivated by Bessel kernels appearing in the Selberg trace formula (spectral-side K_{ir}) and Mellin-Barnes representations, where they could in principle donate an identity that elementary functions cannot. PSLQ at mpmath 50 dps for x ∈ {5000, 10000, 50000, 100000}, maxcoeff=10⁶, maxsteps=8000, with cross-validation at x+1000. Every fit produces tight residuals (1.9e-36 to 4.0e-35) with non-zero coeff(f) ∈ {2712, 5349, 10385}, but **all fail cross-validation by 4–5 orders of magnitude** (cross-check residuals 1.10e+04 / 6.03e+04). Random-control test: replacing f with same-σ Gaussian (σ ≈ 4.23) produces statistically indistinguishable signatures (fit residuals 8.5e-37 to 6.4e-35; cross-check 1.77e+04 to 1.60e+05). The Bessel basis carries no more identity-bearing structure for f(x) than a Gaussian random number; PSLQ's "tight relations" at finite precision are pure numerical coincidences. Rules out three concrete Bessel-shaped donors: Selberg-trace shadow at low order, partition-saddle reuse via K_0(2π√·), and modified-Bessel growth/decay scaffolding. Adds 23rd pseudorandomness measure (Bessel-basis PSLQ indistinguishability from Gaussian). Strengthens S29 verdict from "f(x) admits no identity in tested elementary basis" to "f(x) admits no identity in any basis tested including spectral-trace-related Bessel kernels." Cites E1.x, E3.x, E7.x. See experiments/algebraic/identity_search/bessel_basis_pslq.py and bessel_basis_pslq_results.md. | 68 |
    753:| Free cumulants of the chi_P MPS unfolding operator — composition C2 of E2.1 + free probability | REFINEMENT | E | Construction succeeded as a moment-level refinement of E2.1 plus a cross-domain import (free probability, Mingo-Speicher 2017); no polylog opening. Built A_chi^pos = M^(j) M^(j)^T as a positive self-adjoint operator under tracial state τ = (1/W^j) tr; computed first 4 free cumulants from non-crossing-partition moment-cumulant relation κ_4 = m_4 − 4m_1 m_3 − 2m_2² + 10m_1² m_2 − 5m_1⁴. Verified MP(c) standardized free-cumulant identity κ_r = c^{r-1} on a Gaussian iid (4000, 1000) ensemble before deployment. Across W ∈ {2, 3, 5, 6, 30}, d up to 22 (W=2), 12 (W=3), 8 (W=5,6), 4 (W=30), the chi_P MPS spectrum decomposes into three regimes: **(i)** finite "structural peak" reproduced by matched-active-Bernoulli baseline (rank-1 mean + φ(W)−1 residue indicators); **(ii)** spike band of `O(N^{0.42})` outlier eigenvalues absent from baseline — adaptive outlier count k*(W,d) defined as smallest k s.t. drop_k bulk free cumulants match MP(c=φ(W)/W) within 10% relative, observed `k*/√rank` ≈ 0.6–1.6 (drifting upward from 0.62 at W=2 d=14 to 1.56 at W=2 d=22; fitting `k* ∝ R^{0.85}`); **(iii)** MP bulk matching `c = φ(W)/W = ∏_{p≤W}(1 − 1/p)` (the **Mertens product**) exactly. Three pre-stated falsification predictions: PR1 (chi_P drop_0 within 10% of MP(0.5)) FAIL at small drop, PASS after adaptive drop; PR2 (active-Bernoulli matches MP) PASS from drop_1; PR3 (chi_P bulk matches MP after finite projection across all W) PASS. **Net new content**: (a) the MP-bulk free-Poisson rate of chi_P's MPS unfolding equals the Mertens product φ(W)/W — a new identification of a free-probabilistic invariant with a sieve density; (b) spike-count regularity `k*(W,d) ∝ R^{0.85}` — a structural fact beyond E2.1's rank statement; (c) verified MP(c) standardized cumulant identity. **Algorithmic implication**: any spectral compression of M^(j) faithful at the second-moment level (i.e., reproducing κ_2) needs rank Ω(N^{0.42}) — recovers a polynomial-in-N barrier from a free-probabilistic angle, parallel to (not stronger than) Lagarias-Odlyzko O(N^{2/3}) and the explicit-formula O(N^{1/2+ε}) bounds. Cites E2.1; cross-domain reference Mingo & Speicher 2017 Ch. 1-2, Ch. 4. See experiments/constructions/free_cumulants_chi_p/. | 74 |
    754:| §A.A3 ATTACK_VECTORS — spectral primality test via fixed-generator (Z/nZ)* Cayley graph (cross-domain: spectral graph theory, Bourgain) | FAIL | E | Frontier attack on whether spec(Cay((Z/nZ)*, {2,3,5,2⁻¹,3⁻¹,5⁻¹})) decides primality of n. Empirical: 533 values n ∈ [7,2000] coprime to 30 (300 primes, 13 prime powers, 214 semiprimes, 6 triple-prime composites); 24 spectral features tested (gap, mults, integer-eigenvalue count, trace moments, max multiplicity, entropy). **No feature is disjoint primes-vs-composites.** Mann-Whitney AUC for primes-vs-prime-powers (the hard sub-case): 0.509 with {2,3,5}, 0.566 with {2,3,7}, 0.673 with {2,3,5,7}∪inv — essentially noise. **Structural reason** (proved from CRT character theory + cross-checked on 342 n × 3 generator sets, 0 violations): for n odd squarefree-or-not coprime to S, n_int_eigs(Cay) ≥ **2^ω(n)** (each (Z/p_i^{a_i}Z)* contributes 2-torsion of order 2; product over distinct prime factors). Lower bound is sharp in 84/533 cases. **Spectrum probes ω(n), not primality**: prime n and prime power n=p^k both have ω=1, so 2^ω = 2, and (Z/p^kZ)* is cyclic for odd p (Gauss), structurally identical to (Z/qZ)* for prime q with appropriately aligned discrete logarithms. (E) Spectral feature reduces to ω(n) extraction; deciding primality from ω(n) requires also a perfect-power test, which inherits AKS-class growing-dim MPOW depth (E5.3, E7.10). **Distinct from prior Cayley graph closures** (CLOSED_PATHS lines 354, 383, 384, 385) which used primes-as-generators (circular); A3 fixed generators and varied n. **Net new content**: (a) explicit lower bound n_int_eigs ≥ 2^ω(n) with character-theoretic proof; (b) negative-shape edge candidate: any fixed-generator abelian Cayley graph spectrum is blind to prime-vs-prime-power; (c) generator-robustness empirically verified across {2,3,5}, {2,3,7}, {2,3,5,7}. Cites E5.3, E5.8, E7.10. See experiments/circuit_complexity/cayley_spectral_primality/. | 79 |
    764:| §C4 ATTACK_VECTORS — Anderson localisation Lyapunov exponent gamma(E) of chi_P-driven discrete 1D Schrödinger operator (cross-domain: Aizenman-Warzel random operators, Furstenberg-Kifer theorem) | FAIL | E | Frontier attack on whether chi_P deviates from random pseudorandomness on a SPECTRAL-GLOBAL statistic (Lyapunov exponent) — orthogonal to all 36 prior pseudorandomness measures (which are local correlation / entropy / Fourier / tensor rank / Gowers). Setup: H psi(n) = -psi(n+1) - psi(n-1) + V(n)psi(n), transfer matrix T_n = [[V(n)-E, -1],[1,0]] in SL(2,R), gamma(E) = lim (1/N) log ||T_N..T_1||. Pastur-Figotin perturbation for sparse V in {0,1}: gamma(E) ~ rho(1-rho)/(8 sin^2 k), E = -2 cos k in (-2, 2). Vectorised numerical Lyapunov estimator with periodic L^2 renormalisation, O(N) per energy. **Naive Bernoulli baseline at N=10^5, 50 seeds, 51 energies**: gamma_prime(E) deviates from gamma_bern_mean(E) by max |z| = 88.5 sigma at E = 0.108 (k = pi/2 parity resonance). Z-score grows as sqrt(N): 12.3 at N=10^4 → 88.5 at N=10^5. **Confounder: chi_P concentrated on odd indices.** Parity-matched controls C1 (random subset of odd indices size pi(N)-1 plus V(2)=1) and C3 (chi_P shuffled within odd indices) reduce deviation but still leave 33 sigma at E=1.088 ~ -2 cos(2pi/3) = +1, the **mod-3 resonance** (free transfer matrix rotates 2pi/3 per step, maximally couples to mod-3 periodic potentials; chi_P has mod-3 structure since primes >3 are +-1 mod 3). **W-trick cascade** (random potential supported on {n : gcd(n, W) = 1}, plus delta at small primes p|W): max |z| = 33 (W=2) → 12 (W=6) → 6.3 (W=30) → 6.1 (W=210) → 4.0 (W=2310, N=2*10^5). Cascade ends at residual ~ 4 sigma, borderline noise (Bonferroni 0.05/31 → z_thresh ≈ 3.16). **Same structural reason as E2.13/S85** (Gowers U^k of chi_P → Hardy-Littlewood singular series, recovered by W-trick): chi_P deviation is fully captured by small-modulus residue-class structure. Adds new EDGE E2.14: gamma(E) of Schrödinger operator with V=chi_P deviates from random Lyapunov by a quantity that W-tricks down to noise. **Closure mode E**: deviation IS the spectral signature of Hardy-Littlewood / Bombieri-Vinogradov equidistribution mod q; no additional spectral structure beyond what equidistribution already gives; algorithmic gamma extraction costs Theta(N), not polylog. **Cross-domain ingredient performed real work**: transfer-matrix product / Lyapunov machinery (Aizenman-Warzel 2015 *Random Operators*, Furstenberg-Kifer 1983) had never been applied to chi_P; the spectral signature of the W-trick at the Anderson-Lyapunov level is novel. **PROVIDES SECOND INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION** (after E2.13/S85) that chi_P structure = HL equidistribution mod q, in a category orthogonal to additive combinatorics. Cites E2.13, E1.10, E3.13, E7.1. Cross-domain refs: Aizenman-Warzel 2015 (AMS GSM 168); Furstenberg-Kifer 1983 Israel J. Math. 46; Pastur-Figotin 1992; Green-Tao arXiv:math/0606088 (W-trick). See experiments/dynamical/anderson_localisation_chi_p/anderson_localisation_chi_p_results.md. | 88 |
    770:| §G1 ATTACK_VECTORS — Liouville Anderson Lyapunov: spectral signature of Möbius pseudorandomness (cross-domain: Anderson localisation [reused S88] + Möbius/nilsequence orthogonality, Green-Tao 2012 *Annals* 175 = arXiv:0807.1736; Sarnak 2010 IAS lectures; Tao 2016 logarithmic-Chowla = arXiv:1509.05422) | FAIL | E | First wild-swing attack into the multiplicative regime beyond the W-trick wall. Setup identical to S88/E2.14 except potential is the **centered multiplicative encoding** V(n) = lambda(n) ∈ {-1, +1} (mean → 0 by PNT for Liouville, variance = 1 exactly, NOT the {0, 1} encoding S88 used). Pastur-Figotin prediction in-band collapses to gamma_PF(E) = 1/(8 sin^2 k), exactly matching i.i.d. Rademacher ±1 baseline. Question: any sustained |z| > 5 deviation gamma_lambda(E) - gamma_Rademacher(E) at any energy, NOT W-tricked away — would be A-grade Möbius-side spectral non-orthogonality. **Empirical at 51 energies in E ∈ [-2.95, 2.95], three sample sizes: max |z| = 1.78 (N=10^5, 50 seeds, argmax E=-0.236), 2.16 (N=3·10^5, 50 seeds, argmax +0.118), 2.04 (N=10^6, 100 seeds, argmax -2.006).** All below 51-energy Bonferroni threshold z = 3.16. Argmax energy WANDERS between runs (statistical, not arithmetic). chi^2 / K = 0.49-0.69 (Rademacher prediction = 1; lambda is sub-Rademacher). L^2 distance from Rademacher mean: lambda lies at empirical p ≈ {0.58, 0.86, 0.59} of the seed-to-seed Rademacher distribution. Pastur-Figotin agreement: gamma_lambda / gamma_PF = 0.9317 (std 0.32) inside band, identical to gamma_Rademacher_mean / gamma_PF = 0.9309 to 4 decimals. **Independent off-spectral check (two-point Chowla aggregate at N=10^6, h=1..16): Σ z_h^2 = 4.77 vs Rademacher chi^2_16 mean 16, std sqrt(32) ≈ 5.7** — empirical p ≈ 0.997, lambda is *more Rademacher-like than Rademacher* on Chowla. All 16 individual lag z's |z_h| ≤ 1.11. **Stark contrast with E2.14 (chi_P, S88) at same N=10^5 grid: chi_P max |z| = 88.5σ at locked E=0.108 (parity resonance), required W=2310 sieve to reduce to ~4σ; lambda needs no W-trick at all and |z| flat across two orders of N magnitude.** **Mechanism (structural reason for closure mode E):** Möbius/nilsequence orthogonality (Green-Tao 2012, arXiv:0807.1736) predicts lambda is asymptotically orthogonal to all nilsequences including periodic ones; Lyapunov exponent of det-1 transfer-matrix Markov chain on PSL(2,R) is determined by Furstenberg-Kifer measure, a non-abelian analogue of GT nilsequence framework. lambda's centered multiplicativity destroys the mod-q resonance that drives chi_P's 88σ deviation. Sub-Rademacher Chowla aggregate (Σ z² = 4.77 ≪ 16) is consistent with Tao 2016 logarithmic-Chowla theorem (arXiv:1509.05422). **First non-W-tricked spectral measurement** in the project's 38-measure pseudorandomness battery to land at noise floor without sieving. **Pairs with E2.14 to ISOLATE the source of chi_P's Anderson-Lyapunov deviation as exclusively HL singular-series mod-q resonance:** lambda is the canonical chi_P-twin (density 1/2, centered, multiplicative) without mod-q resonance, so it has no spectral signal. **Closes the intended polylog opening:** if lambda-Lyapunov had detectable structure, the explicit-formula μ-side Σ Li(x^ρ) μ_ρ could plausibly be partial-summed in polylog. lambda is featureless ⇒ explicit-formula Möbius side is irreducible at this measurement scale. **Adds EDGE E2.18**: gamma_lambda(E) ≈ gamma_Rademacher(E) within seed noise across [-3, 3], flat in N up to 10^6, no W-trick needed. **Cross-domain ingredient (Möbius/nilsequence orthogonality) performed real work**: first project use; status in CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md upgraded UNUSED → USED E. Cites E2.14, E2.13, E1.10, E3.13. Cross-domain refs: Aizenman-Warzel 2015 *Random Operators* AMS GSM 168; Furstenberg-Kifer 1983; Pastur-Figotin 1992; Green-Tao arXiv:0807.1736 (Möbius nilsequence orthogonality, primary); Sarnak 2010 IAS lectures (Möbius randomness conjecture); Tao 2016 Forum Math Pi 4 e8 = arXiv:1509.05422 (logarithmic-Chowla); Tao-Teräväinen 2017 arXiv:1708.02610. See experiments/dynamical/liouville_anderson_lyapunov/liouville_anderson_lyapunov_results.md. | 100 |
